WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Let’s talk about the fight over the South China Sea.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.640
It’s rich in oil and gas, there are loads of fish

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.760
and the shipping lanes are some of the busiest 

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.560
in the world.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.200
China wants control of it. 

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.520
But other countries say they have rights to it too. 

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.640
And the Americans — well they have trade routes

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.360
through there worth hundreds of billions of dollars.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.720
So what’s behind China’s claim over these waters?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.960
Why won’t it share? 

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.440
And could it all lead to war?

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.920
China has been a land-based power for millennia

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.880
so it’s never had 
much of a navy

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.280
so it’s never had 
much of a navy

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.360
until now.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.160
Today China wants to be a naval force across two oceans

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.840
the Indian and the Pacific.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.160
That’s to make
 sure it can trade 

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.000
with the rest of the world and get enough oil and gas

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.600
to drive its economy.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.480
And to do that 

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.400
Beijing wants to control
 the South China Sea.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.040
But there’s a lot of competition.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.200
Taiwan, for one, claims 
many of the same rights 

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.360
that China does.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.760
Then there’s Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia 

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.760
and Brunei 

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.240
they want to
 protect their turf 

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.200
whether it’s rights to islands, seabed oil or fishing grounds.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.720
Indonesia’s in there too.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:33.920
And the Americans

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.560
they want to protect those valuable shipping lanes.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.480
So no shortage of players. 

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.600
The question is how is this all going to be resolved?

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.040
And then there’s the third option which involves 

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.120
pushing some kind of compromise between

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.080
China and its neighbours.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.600
Countries like Taiwan and others  

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.760
that are members of ASEAN have tried.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.280
But no one expects that to go anywhere anytime soon 

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.320
because China knows 
what it wants.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.280
And it’s been working towards that for a long time.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.000
We’re talking about 
China’s nine dash line. 

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.560
Back in 1929 the Chinese took a bunch of old British  

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.480
sailing charts and told cartographers to draw 

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.880
China’s borders.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.680
One of them drew nine dashes

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.280
around the entire 
South China Sea.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.160
And as far as the Chinese were concerned that was that.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.280
So then in 2013 China added a tenth line to take in Taiwan.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.120
So then in 2013 China added a tenth line to take in Taiwan.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.440
Its appetite seems 
to be growing.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.560
It’s easy enough to understand why China  

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.960
wants control when you
 look at the numbers.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.480
Around 40% of the world’s liquefied natural gas 

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.280
passes through the South China Sea — from the Gulf

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.760
through the Indian Ocean and on to places like China.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.800
The amount of trade that 
sails through there 

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.440
is worth $3 trillion a year — one-third of the world total.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.040
So there’s an expression in Latin that goes:

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.520
If you want peace 
prepare for war.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.280
Now China has no immediate interest in war 

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.360
but it wants to ensure its access to the South China Sea

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.840
if it comes to that.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.280
In the west the main 
shipping lane 

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.480
from the Indian Ocean narrows to just 3 km.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.960
And it’s surrounded by American allies.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.720
In the east there’s Taiwan

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.920
which has tonnes of 
American weapons

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.480
and could block China from getting to the Pacific.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.840
So to help stake its 
territorial claim 

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.720
China has planted its flag on contested islands 

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.680
that are in those waterways.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.680
In the Paracels China’s built a bunch of outposts 

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.520
while in the Spratlys it’s 
added over 13 sq km

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.160
of reclaimed land since 2013 

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.640
and turned them into 
military bases.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.680
It may be unacceptable 
  to the Americans

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.560
but neither the Obama nor the Trump administrations

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.560
are seen to have 
accomplished much.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.240
The US’ main way of asserting navigational rights  

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.720
and freedoms is having its navy sail 

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.440
through the South China Sea.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.280
It also conducts military exercises in the region

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.400
sometimes with warships from France, Japan

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.200
and Australia in 
the Bay of Bengal.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.600
In July things got a bit hairy.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.680
The Chinese and Americans both held naval exercises

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.760
in the South China Sea.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.360
The US sailed in with two aircraft carrier strike groups.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.840
And the Chinese 
accused the Americans 

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:06.560
of escalating things. 

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.520
Before the US elections 
the Trump team

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.280
gave it one last shot — with mixed results.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.320
What everyone’s been trying to do is get China 

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.120
to obey the law — namely 
the UN Convention  

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.800
on the Law of the Sea.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.200
The law establishes
 what are called 

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.120
exclusive economic zones. 

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.280
These extend 
200 nautical miles 

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.960
out from a country’s coastline 

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.920
regardless of whether it’s the mainland or an island.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.840
And China signed 
up to that law.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.440
So the Philippines did the only thing it could.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:07.640
It took China to court.   

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.240
One of the big issues was fishing rights.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.840
In 2013 the Philippines argued that China had no right

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.320
to the Scarborough Shoal.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.280
It filed a case 
against the Chinese 

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.560
with an international 
tribunal at The Hague.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.960
Judges ended up siding 
with the Philippines.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.960
Remember China’s
 territorial claim based  

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.880
on that nine dash line?

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.240
Well in 2016 the court concluded that it’s invalid.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.600
Since the 1990s ASEAN 

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.960
that association of South Asian countries

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.480
has tried to cobble together a Code of Conduct

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.240
for the South China Sea. 

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:13.320
The idea was to control China’s ambitions. 

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.440
China hadn’t shown much interest in a code back then.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.800
But after losing the case 
 with the Philippines

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.920
the Chinese started 
 to push for one. 

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.720
Though some in ASEAN questioned their motives.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.480
There’s another problem. 

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:44.360
Not everyone in ASEAN is eager to offend a country

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:45.920
as powerful as China.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.440
Vietnam has repeatedly butted heads with China

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.040
over their competing claims.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.680
This year’s ASEAN summit was hosted by Vietnam 

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.240
and a lot of it by video because of the pandemic.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.520
But the social distancing wasn’t expected to help

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.200
delegates reach any agreements  

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:37.000
on the South China Sea.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:48.120
The problem with the 
South China Sea 

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.960
is that it’s vast and the 
stakes are high.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:52.840
There are a lot of heavily armed countries

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:54.800
defending their 
interests there.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.440
And China’s coastguard as well as Chinese militia

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.400
using fishing boats often cause confrontations  

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.040
on the open sea.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.800
Many analysts worry about all of that provoking

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.040
a military response
 from someone.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.160
But strangely enough 
the whole dispute

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:12.080
also sort of just bubbles 
away under the radar.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.440
The Chinese see the 
South China Sea

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:29.960
as essential to their survival.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.320
Thing is it’s important to everyone there. 

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.800
That’s why this whole dispute isn’t going away.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.640
But China’s been around for thousands of years. 

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.280
If anyone is good at playing the long game, it’s China.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.280
As with all our Start Here episodes this primer 

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.640
is to get us started.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.600
But there’s a lot more information out there to help

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:53.640
us understand what’s going on in the South China Sea.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.240
As always subscribe wherever you’re watching this video 

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.360
and I’ll see you next week.

